Why AGPL-3.0
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

REau's Android Auto receiver is derived from AGPLv3-licensed code (see
"Incorporated code" below). Under the terms of that license, the combined work
is distributed under the AGPL-3.0. Anyone who redistributes REau (or runs a
modified version as a network-accessible service) must make the complete
corresponding source available under the same license and preserve the
copyright and attribution notices in this file and in the source files.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Incorporated code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each file copied from a third-party project keeps the origin header it came
with, or carries one naming the project, the license, the path and the commit it
was copied from. The code that comes from EstrelaAustral is listed in the single
notice below instead of a header in each file.

EstrelaAustral (Apache License 2.0)
  Copyright 2026 Murkiriel, on a base from NorthStar
  (Copyright 2026 Aditya Dasika, Apache License 2.0).
  https://github.com/Murkiriel/EstrelaAustral
  Parts of REau are copied or adapted from EstrelaAustral: the build setup (the
  Gradle version catalog entries, the detekt tasks and config/detekt/detekt.yml),
  the ride log (data/RideDiagnostics.kt), the dash link (the
  packet layer, commands and button events in dash/protocol/, the authentication,
  sockets, session, telemetry events, Wi-Fi and the Wi-Fi suggestion in dash/,
  util/Dbg.kt, util/DiagnosticsExport.kt), the
  encrypted preferences (data/KeystoreBackedPrefs.kt), the dash video rules and
  encoder (dash/video/), the stream pacing rules (stream/DashLogic.kt,
  AutoFpsController.kt, AutoKeyframeController.kt, dash/DashConfig.kt), the
  foreground service pattern (service/ProjectionService.kt and its locks and
  notification), the maneuver glyphs (dash/nav/DashManeuverGlyphs.kt,
  dash/nav/ManeuverType.kt) and the tests
  of those parts, including the fake dash used by the instrumented tests. Each
  copied file is changed only in its package name unless REau's own history says
  otherwise. EstrelaAustral's own files carry portions that come from other
  projects (NorthStar, better-dash, recon-dash and open-dash, each with its own
  entry below); they come into REau with those files.

OpenCfMoto (GNU Affero General Public License v3.0)
  Copyright (C) 2026 Alexandru <https://alexandru.rocks> and the OpenCfMoto
  contributors. https://github.com/zanderp/open-cfmoto
  - Android Auto margins: the rule in video/AaMargins.kt (AaMargins.forAspect)
    is adapted from app/src/main/java/dev/zanderp/opencfmoto/BikeProfile.kt,
    commit 0abbe2a. It takes the coded frame's size as two numbers instead of
    OpenCfMoto's AaVideoSpec; contentRect and the tests (AaMarginsTest) are
    new. DashGlass.kt and FrameFit.kt are new code.
  - Compositor: video/CompositorGl.kt and video/DashCompositor.kt are derived
    from app/src/main/java/dev/zanderp/opencfmoto/AaCompositor.kt, commit
    0abbe2a. Kept: the dedicated GL thread, the EGL setup, the
    SurfaceTexture input that exists before any output, the shader program
    and the idea of repeating the last picture when Android Auto goes quiet.
    Changed: free source and destination rectangles instead of fit modes, a
    clock of its own instead of drawing when a frame arrives, the video clock
    as presentation time, black before the first picture; the phone preview,
    the touch mapping, the mirror path and the app dependencies are cut. Each
    file's header lists this. video/CompositorMath.kt and the tests
    (CompositorMathTest, DashCompositorTest, with TestPattern and
    H264TestDecoder) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, message layer (package aa/), copied from
    app/src/main/java/dev/zanderp/opencfmoto/aa/, commit 0abbe2a: the
    generated protobuf classes (aa/proto/Common, Control, Input, Media,
    MediaPlayback, NavigationStatus, Sensors, Wireless), AapMessage.kt,
    AapMessageIncoming.kt, AapMessageHandler.kt, Messages.kt, MsgType.kt,
    Channel.kt, ByteArrayWithLimit.kt, Utils.java, MediaAck.kt,
    VideoFocusEvent.kt, DrivingStatusEvent.kt, NightModeEvent.kt, AaLog.kt,
    AapSsl.kt and AccessoryConnection.kt. Only the Java package differs,
    except AaLog.kt (logcat tag, comments, a Locale for String.format). The
    tests (AapMessageTest, AapProtocolTablesTest) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, transport (package aa/), copied from
    the same folder and commit: AapSslContext.kt, SingleKeyKeyManager.kt,
    NoCheckTrustManager.kt, ConscryptInitializer.kt, AapRead.kt,
    AapReadSingleMessage.kt, AapReadMultipleMessages.kt,
    SocketAccessoryConnection.kt and AapTransport.kt. AapTransport no longer
    builds the video path or the message handler (its session's builder passes
    the handler to startReading) and has no microphone hook; AapRead's
    factory takes the handler; each file's header lists its changes.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, control (package aa/), from the same
    folder and commit: AapControl.kt and AapMessageHandlerType.kt, copied.
    AapControl no longer calls OpenCfMoto's AaVideoBridge, answers a
    microphone request itself (as a microphone that did not open) and builds
    the service discovery response from an AaConfig; AapMessageHandlerType is
    given the video path as a function. aa/ServiceDiscoveryResponse.kt is
    rewritten here after OpenCfMoto's file of the same name (which services
    are made of which messages, and its findings about the audio sink and the
    microphone); its header says what it keeps. aa/AaConfig.kt and the tests
    (ServiceDiscoveryResponseTest, AapControlTest) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, video (package aa/), from the same
    folder and commit: VideoDecoder.kt, copied. Its stall watchdog's rule is
    now aa/DecoderStallWatch.kt (new): the wait is measured from the first
    unanswered input after an output or a pause instead of from the last
    picture, input the phone offered counts whether or not the codec took it,
    and the watchdog begins when a decoder opens instead of with its first
    picture; the test for a hardware codec by name, for Android below 10, is
    cut. aa/AapVideo.kt is rewritten here after OpenCfMoto's file of the same
    name (the reassembly buffer, the two payload offsets, the start-code
    test, the 1 s spacing of keyframe requests) and open-headunit's (below);
    its header says what it keeps. aa/AaVideoSink.kt and the tests
    (AapVideoTest, DecoderStallWatchTest, VideoDecoderTest, AaVideoSinkTest,
    with TestH264Source) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, the link (package aa/): aa/AaReceiver.kt
    is written here after OpenCfMoto's file of the same name (same folder and
    commit). Kept: dialling out to Android Auto's head unit server on
    127.0.0.1:5277 with an 800 ms connect timeout and 2 s between dials, one
    session at a time, and the order of a session (handshake, decoder
    surface, read loop). Not here: the listening socket on 5288 with its NSD
    registration and the AaSelfMode pokes, the 20-dial limit, BikeWifi's
    process-wide network bind, AaVideoBridge, the microphone, the input sinks
    and the night timer (day and night are given to the receiver by the
    service, AaReceiver.setNight). The link states and the tests
    (AaReceiverTest, AaReceiverNightTest) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, input and microphone (package aa/ and
    phone/): aa/AaInput.kt is copied from the same folder and commit (one finger
    from the phone's screen instead of multi-touch; the key codes live in
    ServiceDiscoveryResponse). aa/AaMicrophone.kt and phone/PhoneVoice.kt are
    written here after OpenCfMoto's aa/AaMicrophone.kt (same commit): the
    request/answer order, 20 ms chunks of 16 kHz mono PCM and the media data
    message are kept; the capture sits behind VoiceSource and prefers the
    Bluetooth headset. Each file's header says what it keeps and changes.
  - Thermal ceiling: stream/ThermalCeiling.kt is written here after
    AdaptivePolicy.thermalFpsCap in
    app/src/main/java/dev/zanderp/opencfmoto/AdaptiveVideoController.kt,
    commit 0abbe2a. Kept: four steps by the phone's thermal status, never
    above the base. Changed: the steps are fractions of the rider's ceiling
    (3/4, 5/8, 1/2) instead of OpenCfMoto's absolute rates; its header says
    so. ThermalCeilingTest is new.
  - res/raw/aa_cert and res/raw/aa_privkey, copied byte for byte from
    app/src/main/res/raw/ at the same commit: the head-unit certificate and
    key ("Google-Android-Reference") that open Android Auto receivers present
    in the TLS handshake. They are not this project's and not OpenCfMoto's
    work; they come down the headunit lineage.
  - The tests (AapReadTest, AapSslHandshakeTest, AapTransportTest) and the
    fake Android Auto peer they run against (src/sharedTest:
    FakeAndroidAutoPeer, TestCertificate, with FakeAndroidAutoPeerTest) are
    new.

